<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Install the client packages, connect to the Opal server and choose a resource.
</div>

## Before you start

The book uses an Opal server at `https://localhost:8443`. Its administrator has
already loaded the data as OMOP CDM resources and installed dsOMOP and dsBase on
its R server. You work only on the client side: you send requests and receive
aggregate results, never patient rows. The data are synthetic, so the results
teach methods; they are not clinical findings.

You need R 4.1 or newer. Install the client packages once:

```{r install-packages, eval = FALSE}
install.packages(c("remotes", "DSI", "DSOpal"))
remotes::install_github("datashield/dsBaseClient@6.3.5", upgrade = "never")
remotes::install_github("isglobal-brge/dsOMOPClient@2.10.5", upgrade = "never")
```

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

## Connect to the Opal server

`ds.omop.login()` logs in to the server and attaches one OMOP CDM resource:

```{r login, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.gibleed", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r login-local, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("gibleed")$conns
```

Use the user name and password your administrator gave you, and keep them out of
shared scripts; with a personal access token, pass `token = "<token>"` instead.
`omop.gibleed` is the resource: project `omop`, resource `gibleed`. `site` is the
name this book gives the connection. `conns` holds the connection, not patient rows;
every later call sends its request through it.

## Check the client and the server

```{r versions}
ds.omop.client.version()
ds.omop.server.version(conns = conns)[c("server", "published", "running", "ready")]
```

The first line is the client package on your computer. `ready` confirms that the
server's installed dsOMOP and its available methods agree.

## The resources on the server

Each chapter names the resource it uses. All of them are in the project `omop`:

| Resource | Contents |
|:---|:---|
| `omop.gibleed` | GiBleed: 2,694 synthetic people with visits, conditions and medicines; no numeric lab values |
| `omop.synthea` | Synthea27Nj: 28 synthetic people with numeric lab values and units |
| `omop.questionnaire` | A synthetic questionnaire study of 48 people, described below |
| `omop.questionnaire_vocab` | The questionnaire with a richer teaching vocabulary: codes, synonyms and hierarchies |
| `omop.questionnaire_drugs` | The questionnaire vocabulary plus medicine supply histories |
| `omop.questionnaire_formulas` | The questionnaire plus serum creatinine and repeated same-day weights |
| `omop.questionnaire_site2` | A second site's questionnaire, with different answers and wording |
| `omop.synthea_north`, `omop.synthea_south` | Synthea27Nj split into two sites with different people |
| `omop.gibleed_north`, `omop.gibleed_south` | GiBleed split into two sites with different people |

GiBleed and Synthea27Nj are the public OHDSI Eunomia datasets. Analyst results
report counts in five-person bands, so you will see 2,690 rather than 2,694.

The questionnaire study has four groups of twelve people. Two groups answer the
education question twice, swapping Secondary and University between their first
and last answers; they also answer a yes/no fever question. One group has only a
birth year; one has neither visits nor a death record. Knowing this design lets
you predict the results of later chapters:

| Item | Total | Female | Male |
|:---|---:|---:|---:|
| People | 48 | 20 | 28 |
| Education respondents | 24 | 10 | 14 |
| Education answer rows | 48 | 20 | 28 |
| Fever answer rows | 24 | 10 | 14 |
| Inpatient visits | 24 | 10 | 14 |
| Outpatient visits | 36 | 14 | 22 |
| 2023: myocardial infarction | 12 | 4 | 8 |
| 2023: pneumonia | 6 | 3 | 3 |
| 2024: myocardial infarction | 12 | 4 | 8 |
| 2024: pneumonia | 6 | 3 | 3 |
| Survivors | 12 | 6 | 6 |

There are 24 education respondents but 48 education answer rows: an answer row
is not a person.

## Exercise 1: choose the lab resource

Attach `omop.synthea` instead of GiBleed and request its person count. How can
you tell that you are no longer querying GiBleed?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r numeric-exercise}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.synthea", conns = conns)
ds.omop.table.stats("person", conns = conns)
```

The person count is 25, meaning 25–29 people. That band contains Synthea27Nj's
28 people and is far below GiBleed's 2,694. Chapter 1 explains these bands.
`ds.omop.disconnect()` releases the attached resource; the login stays open.

</details>

## Exercise 2: log in again

End the session, then log in again with GiBleed. Confirm the database with a
person count, rather than checking only that a connection has a name.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r reconnect-exercise, eval = FALSE}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.gibleed", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r reconnect-exercise-local, include = FALSE}
book_disconnect()
conns <- book_connect("gibleed")$conns
```

```{r reconnect-count}
ds.omop.table.stats("person", conns = conns)
```

The count is back to the 2,690–2,694 band. Disconnect releases the resource;
logout closes the DataSHIELD session. A new login starts a new session.

</details>

## Recap

The client packages are installed, `ds.omop.login()` opens a resource on the
server, and `conns` carries your requests.

**Next:** [1. What DataSHIELD and OMOP mean](book-01-mental-model.html).
